The main difference between a bass guitar and a regular guitar is the size and shape of the instrument. A bass guitar is typically much larger than a regular guitar, with longer strings and a deeper sound. It also has four thicker, round strings instead of the six thinner strings found on a regular guitar. The body of a bass guitar is usually made out of solid wood, while regular guitars can have either solid or hollow bodies. Additionally, bass guitars usually have fatter necks that make it easier to play chords and strum chords with both hands. Finally, bass guitars often have extra low "bass" strings which produce deeper sounds than the higher-pitched strings found on regular guitars. Overall, these differences make the bass guitar an ideal instrument for creating low-end sounds and providing a foundation for other instruments.  It is commonly used in genres such as rock, jazz, blues and metal.  It can also be used as an accompaniment instrument in orchestras or bands.  The versatility of the bass guitar makes it an essential part of many musical ensembles today. 
